{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"focal length and field of view focal length and field of view","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md","fileName":"Focal Length and Field of View.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Focal Length and Field of View","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 00:47\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Photogrammetric Image Analysis]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Photogrammetric Image Analysis","fakephotos.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"focal length and field of view focal length and field of view","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Focal Length and Field of View.md","fileName":"Focal Length and Field of View.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Focal Length and Field of View","heading":"Focal Length and Field of View","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Focal length is the distance between the projection center and sensor in the pinhole model, while field of view is the angular extent captured by the camera. For a fixed sensor, increasing focal length magnifies projected objects and narrows the field; decreasing it widens the scene and reduces projected size.\n\nThese linked quantities constrain whether apparent object sizes and perspective are compatible with a claimed camera setup. Cropping can imitate a narrower view without reproducing every optical consequence, so image dimensions and metadata must be considered together.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Photogrammetric Image Analysis","fakephotos.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"p